# Linux File Permission Lab – Understanding Directory and File Permissions

## Lab Objective

Students will learn how **Read (r)**, **Write (w)**, and **Execute (x)** permissions affect access to **directories** and **files** by validating each permission individually.

---

# Lab 1: Create a Student User

Login as root.

```bash
sudo -i
```

Create a user.

```bash
useradd student1
passwd student1
```

Verify.

```bash
id student1
```

---

# Lab 2: Create a Secure Directory

Create the directory.

```bash
mkdir /secure
```

Set only owner permissions.

```bash
chmod 700 /secure
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -ld /secure
```

Expected Output

```text
drwx------  root root  /secure
```

---

# Lab 3: Verify Student Access (No Permissions)

Login as student.

```bash
su - student1
```

Try listing the directory.

```bash
ls /secure
```

Expected

```text
ls: cannot open directory '/secure': Permission denied
```

Try entering the directory.

```bash
cd /secure
```

Expected

```text
-bash: cd: /secure: Permission denied
```

---

# Lab 4: Give Others Read Permission Only

Login as root.

```bash
exit
chmod o+r /secure
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -ld /secure
```

Expected

```text
drwx----r--
```

---

## Validate Read Permission

Login as student.

```bash
su - student1
```

Attempt to list the directory.

```bash
ls /secure
```

Expected

```text
ls: cannot access '/secure': Permission denied
```

### Explanation

> Read permission alone is **not enough** for a directory.
> A user also needs **Execute (x)** permission to access or list its contents.

Try entering the directory.

```bash
cd /secure
```

Expected

```text
Permission denied
```

---

# Lab 5: Add Write Permission (rw-)

Login as root.

```bash
exit
chmod o+rw /secure
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -ld /secure
```

Expected

```text
drwx----rw-
```

---

## Validate Write Permission

Login as student.

```bash
su - student1
```

Try creating a file.

```bash
touch /secure/test.txt
```

Expected

```text
touch: cannot touch '/secure/test.txt': Permission denied
```

### Explanation

Write permission alone cannot create files because **Execute (x)** is still missing.

---

# Lab 6: Add Execute Permission (rwx)

Login as root.

```bash
exit
chmod o+rwx /secure
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -ld /secure
```

Expected

```text
drwx---rwx
```

---

## Validate Execute Permission

Login as student.

```bash
su - student1
```

Enter the directory.

```bash
cd /secure
```

Verify.

```bash
pwd
```

Expected

```text
/secure
```

Create a file.

```bash
touch test.txt
```

Verify.

```bash
ls
```

Expected

```text
test.txt
```

Remove it.

```bash
rm test.txt
```

---

# Lab 7: Create a Confidential File

Login as root.

```bash
exit
```

Create the file.

```bash
touch /secure/confidential
```

Edit the file.

```bash
vi /secure/confidential
```

Press

```text
i
```

Type

```text
Linux Administration
Confidential Information
Do Not Share
```

Save.

```text
Esc
:wq
```

Set permission.

```bash
chmod 600 /secure/confidential
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -l /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
-rw-------
```

---

# Lab 8: Validate File Read Permission

Login as student.

```bash
su - student1
```

Read the file.

```bash
cat /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
cat: /secure/confidential: Permission denied
```

---

## Add Read Permission

Login as root.

```bash
exit
chmod o+r /secure/confidential
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -l /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
-rw----r--
```

Login as student.

```bash
su - student1
```

Read again.

```bash
cat /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
Linux Administration
Confidential Information
Do Not Share
```

---

# Lab 9: Validate File Write Permission

Append text.

```bash
echo "Student Entry" >> /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
Permission denied
```

---

## Add Write Permission

Login as root.

```bash
exit
chmod o+w /secure/confidential
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -l /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
-rw----rw-
```

Login as student.

Append data.

```bash
echo "Student Entry" >> /secure/confidential
```

Verify.

```bash
cat /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
Linux Administration
Confidential Information
Do Not Share
Student Entry
```

---

# Lab 10: Validate Execute Permission

Attempt to execute the file.

```bash
./secure/confidential
```

or

```bash
/secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
Permission denied
```

---

## Add Execute Permission

Login as root.

```bash
exit
chmod o+x /secure/confidential
```

Verify.

```bash
ls -l /secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
-rw----rwx
```

Login as student.

Run the file.

```bash
/secure/confidential
```

Expected

```text
bash: /secure/confidential: Permission denied
```

or

```text
command not found
```

### Explanation

Even though execute permission is set, the file contains **plain text**, not executable code. Execute permission only works for executable binaries or scripts with a valid interpreter (for example, a shell script starting with `#!/bin/bash`).

---

# Learning Outcomes

| Permission | Directory Behaviour                                           | File Behaviour                                      |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| **r**      | View directory entries (requires `x` to actually access them) | Read file contents                                  |
| **w**      | Create, delete, or rename files (requires `x`)                | Modify file contents                                |
| **x**      | Enter (`cd`) and access the directory                         | Execute the file if it is a valid executable/script |

This lab demonstrates why **directory permissions and file permissions behave differently**, which is one of the most important concepts in Linux administration.
